# Reviewed gitleaks exceptions.
#
# Each entry is a specific finding fingerprint — commit, file, rule, line — not
# a pattern and not a path glob, so an exception here can never silently cover
# a different secret. A real leak on another line, or in another file, still
# fails the scan.
#
# Both entries below are synthetic fixtures that exist to prove the platform
# scrubs credentials before they are persisted. Weakening the fixtures would
# weaken the tests, which is why they are excepted rather than rewritten.
#
# Deliberately described rather than quoted: reproducing the literals here
# would create a fresh finding in this very file, which is exactly what
# happened on the first attempt.
#
#   tests/redteam/test_agentic_red_team.py:381
#     Feeds two obviously-synthetic bearer/api-key strings — a sequential
#     alphabet and a sequential digit run — plus the example AWS access key ID
#     published in AWS's own public documentation, through redact_payload, and
#     asserts none of them survives into the audit ledger.
#
#   tests/security/test_tool_gateway.py:300
#     Passes a similarly synthetic key as a tool parameter and asserts the
#     persisted tool_calls row holds only the redacted form.
#
# Neither string is, or ever was, a credential for anything.
